# satellite -- what `make startup` measures, and what it measured last time.
#
# THE REGISTRY, AND make_support/startup.sh IS ITS ONE CONSUMER. PLAN §9 has
# said "startup is re-measured every milestone" since the plan was written and
# nothing implemented it, so M4, M4.5 and M5 each landed without one and a 34x
# regression sat undiscovered for three milestones (MILESTONES/M6.md §9.1). A
# rule with no target behind it is a rule nobody runs. This file is the target's
# data half: adding a command to the measurement is ONE LINE HERE and no edit
# anywhere else, which is the same arrangement words.def has with the trie and
# errors.def has with the reporter.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE THING TO UNDERSTAND BEFORE READING A NUMBER OUT OF THIS FILE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A ROW IS NOT COMPARABLE ACROSS LINK MODES. A SHARE IS.
#
# 040-sources.mk records the mistake that taught this: satl is shipped static,
# and timing it against a DYNAMIC `int main(){return 0;}` made satl look 0.9 ms
# FASTER than an empty program -- a linking difference wearing a performance
# result's clothes. The rule written down at the time was "both sides static".
# That is the right instruction and it is not the underlying fact, which was
# measured on 2026-08-31 while this harness was being built:
#
#                     floor    satl --version    satl's own share
#     dynamic         1.457         1.633              0.176
#     static          0.568         0.755              0.187
#     M6's record     0.556         0.724              0.168
#
# The two absolute columns differ by 0.9 ms between link modes. THE SHARE DOES
# NOT -- 0.176 against 0.187, and against the 0.168 M6 recorded statically on a
# different afternoon. The dynamic loader is a constant this build pays twice,
# once in the floor and once in satl, so subtracting the floor removes it.
#
# So the real rule is BOTH SIDES LINKED THE SAME WAY, and then the share is the
# number a milestone is answerable for. startup.sh links its floor binary
# through the same $(CXX) $(CXXFLAGS) $(LDFLAGS) $(STATIC_LDFLAGS) $(LINK_ENV)
# that 050-build.mk links satl through, so the two sides CANNOT drift apart --
# the mistake above is not one this harness can make. The ms column below is
# still recorded, because a person wants to know what a command costs on a
# machine; it is simply not the column a regression is read from.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FORMAT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. Every other line is
# fields separated by `|`, and the leading word says which kind:
#
#     FLOOR | <ms>              what `int main(){return 0;}` cost last time
#     ROW   | <ms> | <args>     what `satl <args>` cost last time
#     <KEY>   <value>           a condition the baseline was taken under
#
# `|` AND NOT WHITESPACE, so that the empty args field on the bare `satl` row
# is visible rather than being a trailing space somebody's editor strips. A ms
# of `-` means never measured, and startup.sh prints that row with no delta
# rather than inventing one.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BASELINE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DATE    2026-08-31
LINK    full
LOAD    0.88
NOTE    M8 -- satellite.variable.number. MILESTONES/M8.md §7

# THE ROWS ARE THE COMMANDS THAT COST SOMETHING DIFFERENT, not every command
# satl has. --version is the row §9.1 says to read -- it does nothing but say
# what it is, so its share is satl's startup and nothing else. The rest are here
# because each adds one identifiable piece of work on top of that: the trie
# walk, reading and lexing a small file, reading and lexing the largest file in
# the tree, the config reader, and now the whole pipeline.
#
# M7 ADDED 0.011 ms TO satl'S OWN SHARE -- 0.179 against M6's 0.168 -- and that
# is six objects' worth of a larger binary and nothing else. The pass itself
# does not run unless `--resolve` is asked for, so `--version` cannot be paying
# for it; what moved is the size of the image the loader maps. MILESTONES/M7.md
# §8 has the attribution.
#
# M8 ADDED 0.018 ms AND IT IS THE SAME FINDING AGAIN, WITH ONE MORE PIECE OF
# EVIDENCE THAN M7 HAD. Every row moved by about the same amount -- +0.018 to
# +0.036 -- and the row that moved is the BARE `satl`, which prints its opening
# and runs no arm at all. So the cost is paid before any command is chosen and
# cannot be M8's arithmetic. What changed is the image: satl at STATIC=full went
# from 1,828,776 bytes to 1,887,016, which is +58,240 for six number objects and
# the command arm.
#
# AND THE ARITHMETIC ITSELF IS UNDER THE NOISE, WHICH IS THE ROW PAIR BELOW
# SAYING SO. `--number 1 + 1` costs 0.214 against `--version`'s 0.204 -- 0.010
# ms for reading two operands, adding them and printing five lines -- and
# `1 / 3` costs 0.017 more than that for long division to 34 digits and the one
# allocation it boxes into. Measured across three runs of the harness on the
# same build; the spread between runs was 0.027 ms, which is larger than either
# figure and is why the claim above is about the SHAPE of the table rather than
# about one row.

FLOOR | 0.555

ROW | 0.754 |
ROW | 0.759 | --version
ROW | 0.945 | --words
ROW | 0.856 | --tokens example/hello_world.satl
ROW | 1.154 | --tokens example/class_test.satl
ROW | 1.493 | --limits
ROW | 1.512 | --limits example/satellite_config.ini

# M7's ROWS. `--resolve` is the milestone's consumer and it is the first row here
# that is a WHOLE PIPELINE -- lex, parse, resolve, and SATC.md §4's reading order
# around all three -- so it is not comparable to `--tokens`, which stops after
# the first of them.
#
# THEY ARE WARM AND NOT COLD, WHICH IS A PROPERTY OF THE HARNESS AND HAS TO BE
# KNOWN TO READ THEM. startup.sh runs each command 200 times per batch; the
# FIRST of those writes a `.satc` and the other 199 read it, so what these two
# rows measure is a warm run with one cold run's cost averaged into it.
#
# AND MILESTONES/M4.5.md §5's CLAUSE IS NOT READ OUT OF THEM. What the skip
# saves is a trie walk per path, and on a 273-byte program that is far under
# what a shell loop can see -- §5's own table is what says so, and this file's
# baseline note says a row is worth recording because a person wants to know
# what a command costs, not because it proves anything. `satl --resolve` COUNTS
# the walks instead, exactly, and tests/resolve_test/cache.cpp asserts the
# count. On example/frames.satl it is 28 walked cold against 14 walked and 14
# taken warm.
ROW | 0.893 | --resolve example/hello_world.satl
ROW | 1.147 | --resolve example/frames.satl

# M8's ROWS. `--number` is the milestone's consumer and it is the one command
# here that reads nothing from disk -- no file, no `.satc`, no config beyond the
# lookup every run makes -- so its share over `--version` is arithmetic and the
# six new objects and nothing else.
#
# TWO ROWS BECAUSE THE TWO ANSWERS COST DIFFERENT THINGS. `1 + 1` never leaves
# DESIGN §8.2's small form: an add and an overflow check, no allocation. `1 / 3`
# runs long division to division_digits and boxes the result. A single row would
# average the two and hide which half moved.
ROW | 0.769 | --number 1 + 1
ROW | 0.786 | --number 1 / 3
